sort — third-party attribution

This file carries a CONDITION. The sorting code in `src/` is a port of the
Rust standard library's slice sorts, `library/core/src/slice/sort/` at the
Rust 1.93.1 tag (https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/tree/1.93.1/library/core/src/slice/sort),
by Lukas Bergdoll and Orson Peters (ipnsort, driftsort) and the Rust Project
Contributors. Rust is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; the MIT option is
taken, and its notice below must travel with these files.

**Copyleft:** NONE-SHIPPED — the only third-party material is Rust's MIT OR Apache-2.0 source; no GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was read or ported.

What was taken (SOURCE, re-expressed in Zig, MODIFIED: no panics — a broken
order is answered with a permutation instead; raw indices instead of
drop guards, since Zig has no unwinding; caller-supplied scratch for the
stable sort):

  - unstable/mod.rs, unstable/quicksort.rs, unstable/heapsort.rs
                                   → src/unstable.zig (ipnsort)
  - stable/mod.rs, stable/drift.rs, stable/quicksort.rs, stable/merge.rs
                                   → src/stable.zig (driftsort)
  - shared/pivot.rs, shared/mod.rs → src/unstable.zig (choosePivot,
                                     findExistingRun)
  - shared/smallsort.rs            → src/smallsort.zig
  - select.rs                      → src/select.zig (introselect)

The sorting networks in src/smallsort.zig are Bert Dobbelaere's optimal
networks (https://bertdobbelaere.github.io/sorting_networks.html), as listed
in Rust's smallsort.rs — a published table of comparator pairs.

Nothing else is taken: tests, benchmarks and documentation are this
repository's own.

